// TEST: Fold A Grid Returned From An Abstract-Parameter Function
// ============================================================================
// The other half of 155. Same "fold a returned grid" shape, but the producing
// function takes ABSTRACT parameters (`T^1`), and the failure was earlier and
// louder -- IR validation rather than g++:
//
//     error[BL6001]: in binding 'rows': dangling VarId reference: v23
//     error[BL6001]: in binding 'rows': ApplyInfo: kernel slot is IRVar(v23)
//                    [id resolves in neither CallablesTable nor synthetic registry]
//
// WHY. The innermost-axis fold synthesizes a row kernel `__lambda_N(row: T^1)`
// and lifts it into IRModule.Functions. With an abstract-param producer, that
// row type is still open when `monomorphizeHMFunctions` runs, so
// `hasTypeVarsInParams` classifies the kernel as HM-polymorphic and the pass
// DROPS it -- but its call-site collector only recognises
// `IRApp (IRVar (funcId, _), args, _)`, and this callable is named only from
// an IRApplyCombinator's KERNEL SLOT. No spec was ever requested, nothing
// replaced it, and the slot pointed at a deleted function.
//
// The rule is now "keep what nothing replaced", decided on the rewritten
// module: after call sites are repointed at their specs, a surviving
// `IRVar(f.Id)` means a reference specialization could not express, so the
// original is kept and rewritten like any other function (its residual vars
// are the enclosing program's, which the global binding map resolves). A
// genuinely dead abstract original is referenced by nothing and still goes.
//
// The `[t, 2.0 * t]` literal row map is what keeps the kernel's row type open
// long enough to reach the classifier; the literal itself is pinned by 152.
// ============================================================================
function pairs(ts: T^1) = method_for(ts) <@> lambda(t) -> [t, 2.0 * t]

let tim = (method_for(range<Idx<8>>) <@> lambda(i) -> 1.0 * i) |> compute
let cells = pairs(tim) |> compute
// EXPECT: cells = [[0, 0], [1, 2], [2, 4], [3, 6], [4, 8], [5, 10], [6, 12], [7, 14]]

// Innermost-axis fold: t + 2t = 3t per row.
let rows = reduce(cells, (+))
// EXPECT: rows = [0, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21]

// Leading-axis fold over the same operand: column sums over t = 0..7, so
// sum(t) = 28 and sum(2t) = 56.
let cols = reduce(cells, lambda(a: T^1, b: T^1) -> a + b)
// EXPECT: cols = [28, 56]

// And the partial index, 155's witness read through an abstract-param callee.
let row3 = cells(3)
// EXPECT: row3 = [3, 6]
